// Fixture: domain_field_literal_type_mismatch_error
// Rule under test: GDDL Spec v4 §4.2 -- identifier-typed fields are strictly
// domain-bound, "exactly like u32 or string 30 are strict types." Negative path,
// case 1 of 2: a bare literal (not an identifier reference at all) assigned to a
// domain-typed field. Distinct failure mode from domain_field_wrong_domain_error.gddl,
// where the assigned value IS a valid identifier reference, just from the wrong domain
// -- this fixture's value isn't an identifier reference of any kind.
//
// This mirrors the intentional `attack = 1` negative-path case already present in the
// canonical Example.txt (Human_Fighter), but isolated here as its own dedicated,
// minimal fixture rather than relying on that larger, multi-purpose file for this
// specific rule's coverage.

identifier ActionAttack
	melee_weapon  = "Standard attack done with a melee weapon"
	ranged_weapon = "Standard attack done with a ranged weapon"

define Creature
	attack = ActionAttack

Creature Broken
	attack = 1
	// ERROR: 1 is a bare numeric literal, not an ActionAttack domain member.
	// attack is strictly typed to ActionAttack -- no implicit conversion from any
	// other type, including plain integers, is permitted (§4.2, Core Principle 1).

// Expected (prediction, pending golden output from Compiler Core reference run):
//   Compile error against Broken: field `attack` expects an ActionAttack domain
//   member, got a bare literal of the wrong type.
